      "role": "user",
      "content": "Interesting. I like the contrast you're building. It really highlights the evolution.\n\nOne thing, though—I want to make sure the distinction between the two is crystal clear for the younger students. When we talk about the \"Early Human Ingenuity\" groups, can we emphasize that their way of life was more of a natural, almost instinctive way of living? Like, their knowledge was passed down through feeling and tradition rather than through formal, rigorous study? \n\nAnd then for the \"Modern Civilization\" section, can we make it clear that this is where the real intellectual rigor began? Like, where we moved from just reacting to nature to actually understanding the complex, mathematical laws that govern it? I want to show that transition from a more primitive, instinct-based existence to a truly rational, scientific one.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
